  "behavior": [
    "The explicit unsafe EraseLast adapter removes automatic ownership of the last slot without moving its bytes, invokes the destructor callback with its exclusive pointer and a shared borrow of the actual used field, then decrements used.",
    "Metadata borrows are disjoint from block storage. No whole-array alias, mirrored count or copied snapshot is supplied. The native fixture reads owner.Size inside Item::~Item; the Rust Drop implementation reads the scoped used reference installed by the callback.",
    "Six native traces span empty, one-item, same-block and crossing-block cases, repeated erasure, retained-slot reuse and a final empty erasure. Every destructor sees the pre-erasure logical size and the exact original element address.",
    "A guard decrements logical size on unwind and retains uncertainty, while the vector no longer owns the retired slot. Focused Rust-only panic tests prove destructor failure cannot cause a second drop. This is explicitly separate from the native noexcept destruction domain.",
    "The adapter validates canonical initialized block prefixes before transferring destruction responsibility. Hidden initialized tails are rejected without callback invocation or mutation; empty calls do not require item traits or callbacks.",
    "All52 original legacy runtime declarations are joined to normal and strict Miri Rust results; existing initialized-tail panic recovery tests remain green."
  ],
  "limitations": [
    "The existing safe erase_last convenience is unchanged and remains partial for callback-time native Size: it decrements used before truncate. The new explicit adapter covers the native nonpanicking destructor observation phase.",
    "The unsafe callback must end the original lifetime exactly once without freeing block storage and cannot access the element or metadata borrow after returning/unwinding. If it panics before destruction, cleanup remains its responsibility.",
    "The native destroyAt noexcept termination behavior is not emulated by Rust unwinding; recovery tests are Rust extensions.",
    "Only initialized canonical prefixes are covered. Native undefined behavior, whole-owner reentrant mutation, generic destructor ABI and arbitrary ownership corruption are not claimed.",
    "Trivial items use a no-op destruction callback; native trait inference is not automatic in this explicit policy adapter."
  ],
  "global_api_credit_delta": 0,
